          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
            is said to be
            %em commutative
            if its operations are algebra homomorphisms under any interpretation, generalizing the familiar case of the theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoid"} commutative monoids
          %p
            A more general notion is that of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+monads"} monoidal monads
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Two operations,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
            are said to
            %strong commute
            if for any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix"} matrix
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            of elements, with the number of rows given by the arity of
