          %p
            The operations that commute with a given set of operations in an algebraic theory form a subtheory. The
            %strong centre
            of an algebraic theory is given by the operations that commute with all the operations of the theory. An algebraic theory is
            %strong commutative
            if every pair of its operations commute. Another way of describing the centre is to say that it consists of those operations which are also
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
          %p
            This concept may also be formalized in the language of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monad"} commutative monad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
            that admits the construction of a free algebra on any set, so that the theory may be equivalently described by a monad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is automatically strong, i.e., carries a left strength
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_{A,B} \,\colon\, A \times (T B) \longrightarrow T(A \times B)
          %p
            (by
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "strong+monad#example-in-set"} this example
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi τ
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
