          %h3#relation_to_commutativity_of_operations Relation to commutativity of operations
          %p
            A classical example of a commutative algebraic theory is the theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Abelian+groups"} Abelian groups
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eckmann%E2%80%93Hilton+argument"} Eckmann–Hilton argument
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            commutes with itself when
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a \otimes b) \otimes (c \otimes d) = (a \otimes c) \otimes (b \otimes d)
          %p
            and, since the theory of groups is associative and unital, we can take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = d = 1
            and deduce that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi c
                  %mo =
                  %mi c
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi b
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \otimes c = c \otimes b.
          %p
            However, if we suppose
            %em only
            a binary operation that commutes with itself, then the operation is not invariant under a transposition of its arguments, since there are models of that theory like
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi b
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a, b) \mapsto a \otimes b \coloneqq a
            where such invariance is violated.
          %p There is a fairly general situation in which commutativity of an operator with itself implies that the operation is invariant under permutation of arguments.
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              be
              = succeed "-ary" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              be a constant that is a unit for both
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \vdash f(1, \ldots, 1, x, 1, \ldots, 1) = x
              and similarly for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
