            conditions” = “linearity in separate variables” is replaced by “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            \-homomorphicity in separate variables”, where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            of the algebraic theory.
          %p
            In slightly more detail, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            are
            = succeed "-algebras," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \otimes B
            ought to be
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(A \times B)
            modulo equivalences which we may write suggestively as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi χ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∼
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi χ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %mi χ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ;
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi m
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega(a_1, \ldots, a_m) \otimes \chi(b_1, \ldots, b_n) \sim (\omega(\chi, \ldots, \chi); a_1 \otimes b_1, \ldots, a_m \otimes b_n)
          %p where the left side is represented by a composite
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi T
                  %mi B
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ξ
                        %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %msub
                        %mi ξ
                        %mi B
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T A \times T B \stackrel{\xi_A \times \xi_B}{\to} A \times B \stackrel{u(A \times B)}{\to} T(A \times B)
          %p
            (the
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ξ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi T
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi T
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_{A, B} \colon T A \times T B \to T(A \times B).
          %p
            In more detail still,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \otimes B
            is the following
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizer"} coequalizer
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Alg
                      %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Alg_T
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi T
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi T
                          %mi A
                          %mo ×
                          %mi T
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi T
                              %mi α
                        %mtd
                          %mi T
                          %mi T
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi A
                          %mo ×
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mi T
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %msub
                                  %mi ξ
                                  %mi A
                                %mo ×
                                %msub
                                  %mi ξ
                                  %mi B
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi m
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi T
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi A
                          %mo ×
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  T(T A \times T B) &amp; \stackrel{T\alpha}{\to} &amp; T T(A \times B) &amp; \\
                  \&amp; ^\mathllap{T(\xi_A \times \xi_B)} \searrow &amp; \downarrow^\mathrlap{m} &amp; \\
                  \&amp; &amp; T(A \times B) &amp; \to &amp; A \otimes B
                  }
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Seal12"}> Seal 12, section 2.2 and theorem 2.5.5
            )
          %p
            This construction carries over to the wider context of monoidal monads, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+algebras+over+a+commutative+monad"} tensor product of algebras over a commutative monad
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            See (more generally)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monad"} examples of commutative monads
          %h2#related_pages Related pages
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+theory"} tensor product theory
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The notion of commutative algebraic theory was introduced by
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fred+Linton"} Fred Linton
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fred+Linton"}> Fred Linton
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Autonomous equational categories
          %p
            It was formulated in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"} Anders Kock
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Monads on symmetric monoidal closed categories
          %p
            The
            = succeed "-structure" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+category"} closed category
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Strong functors and monoidal monads
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://home.imf.au.dk/kock/SFMM.pdf"} PDF
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
                ,
                %em Closed categories generated by commutative monads
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/KockMonoidalMonads.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %p
            and the
            = succeed "-structure" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
          %ul
            %li#Keigher78
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Keigher"}> William Keigher
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Symmetric monoidal closed categories generated by commutative adjoint monads
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/numdam-bin/fitem?id=CTGDC_1978__19_3_269_0"} NUMDAM
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://archive.numdam.org/article/CTGDC_1978__19_3_269_0.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Seal12
              %p
                Gavin J. Seal,
                %em Tensors, monads and actions
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1205.0101"}> arXiv:1205.0101
                )
          %p
            There is
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/a/75929/381"} related MO discussion
          %p The Kronecker product of theories was introduced in an article of Freyd:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Freyd"}> Peter Freyd
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Algebra valued functors in general and tensor products in particular
          %p
            Recently
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nikolai+Durov"} Nikolai Durov
            rediscovered that notion for the purposes of geometry (under the name
            = succeed ")," do
              %strong commutative algebraic monad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum+%28geometry%29"} spectrum
            of Grothendieck) and theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+scheme+after+Durov"} generalized schemes on this basis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eckmann%E2%80%93Hilton+argument"} Eckmann–Hilton argument
            concerning commutative finitary monads. Much detail including many examples and further constructions are in his thesis
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nikolai+Durov"}> Nikolai Durov
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em New approach to Arakelov geometry
              %a{:href => "http://arXiv.org/abs/0704.2030"} arXiv:0704.2030
        .revisedby
